    "body": "Replacing datetime classes before importing a Cython extension can bind native declarations to Python replacement classes and trigger unbounded recursion between the extension traversal callback and CPython's subtype_traverse() during GC.\n\nAllow the existing type name specification for extern classes to reference a C type object. Retain an owned reference and apply the same type and size validation as ordinary imports. The native datetime declarations use this mechanism to obtain their types from PyDateTime_CAPI; all datetime-specific code is in datetime.pxd. Declarations without a type object keep their existing import behavior.\n\nAdd regressions for patched datetime classes, an invalid capsule, static C type objects, getter errors, non-types, incompatible sizes and reference ownership. Subprocesses forward the runner's import paths to support shared utility builds. Document the extern type-object interface.\n\nLocal C/C++ regressions pass on Windows and Linux, including free-threaded CPython, shared utility builds and the minimum supported CPython 3.9. The generic importer also passes with the Limited API, PyPy and GraalPy; reduced datetime type-identity checks pass on PyPy and GraalPy, with a native getter additionally checked in C/C++ on GraalPy. Compiler unit tests, 283 syntax-error tests, official formatting checks, targeted UBSan checks and compilation with old and newly generated API headers pass. With rebuilt pandas main, the original scenario passes in five fresh processes on Windows (Python main) and Linux (Python 3.15 and main), with empty stderr. Downstream extensions need regeneration and rebuilding. macOS and official CI remain untested locally.\n\nRelated: https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/159408 and https://github.com/pandas-dev/pandas/issues/64023.",
